{"id":"d7799aaa-ef48-485c-b736-632a8277bba8","object":"chat.completion","created":1785002841,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in case[\\\"nodes\\\"]}\\n    required_symbols = case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"]\\n    roots = [n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes.values() if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is None]\\n    if len(roots) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Must have exactly one root\\\")\\n    root_id = roots[0]\\n    children = {}\\n    for nid, n in nodes.items():\\n        p = n[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(p, []).append(nid)\\n    subtree_counts = {}\\n    def compute_counts(nid):\\n        counts = {}\\n        n = nodes[nid]\\n        sym = n[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        counts[sym] = counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n        for cid in children.get(nid, []):\\n            child_counts = compute_counts(cid)\\n            for s, cnt in child_counts.items():\\n                counts[s] = counts.get(s, 0) + cnt\\n        subtree_counts[nid] = counts\\n        return counts\\n    total_counts = compute_counts(root_id)\\n    for req in required_symbols:\\n        if total_counts.get(req, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol '{req}' not present\\\")\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes.values(), key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n    removed_ids = set()\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        if node[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        sub_counts = subtree_counts[nid]\\n        ok = True\\n        for req in required_symbols:\\n            if total_counts[req] - sub_counts.get(req, 0) < 1:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not ok:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = False\\n        for cid in children.get(nid, []):\\n            if cid not in removed_ids:\\n                has_retained_child = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        removed_ids.add(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted([nid for nid in nodes if nid not in removed_ids])\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for SE-PE-01. The problem is about grammar-guided node reduction on a tree. The case contains: nodes (list of dicts with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility), and required_symbols (list of symbols that must be present). We need to produce removed_node_ids (list of IDs in removal order) and retained_node_ids (list of IDs sorted). The objective is to reduce the tree by removing removable nodes while preserving required symbols and maintaining grammar validity? The description mentions \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\", \"grammar_valid\", \"interestingness_preserved\". But the output only specifies removed/retained node IDs. Also \"treat registered properties as hard contracts\" suggests we need to follow a specific algorithm: deeper nodes before shallower, never remove root, required symbol, or node with retained children. Tentatively remove only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as tiebreak at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nThe input payload has nodes with parent references forming a tree. The root has parent null. The tree is likely a parse tree. We need to traverse nodes in some order, decide whether to remove them if removable and condition holds. The algorithm: process nodes in decreasing depth (deeper first), and for nodes at same depth, by some order? Tiebreak by node ID? But description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we need to sort nodes by depth descending, then by node ID ascending (or some order to ensure deterministic). Then for each node, if it is removable, check if removing it would still keep all required symbols present in the tree? Required symbols are a list of symbols that must exist in the retained tree. Also we must ensure that removing a node does not remove a required symbol node? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, it cannot be removed. Also if it has children that are retained (i.e., we haven't removed them yet), we cannot remove it? Because removing a parent would remove its children, so if children are retained, we cannot remove the parent. But since we process deeper first, children might be considered before parents. So when we consider a node, its children have already been processed (possibly removed or retained). So we can check if any of its children are still in the tree (i.e., not removed). If it has any retained children, cannot remove. Also we must ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present somewhere in the tree. That means we need to check if the node's symbol is required, but also if removing this node would cause that symbol to be missing if it's the only occurrence? Actually required_symbols is a list of symbols that must be represented in the final tree. So if a node is the only node with that symbol, removing it would violate requirement. So we need to check that after removing this node, for each required symbol, there exists at least one node in the remaining tree (including nodes not yet processed? But since we are processing in order, we can simulate removal by checking if the number of nodes with that symbol after removal is >=1. So we can count occurrences of each symbol in the current full tree, then for each node we consider removal, check if the count of that symbol minus the contribution of this node (and its descendants? Actually removing a node also removes all its descendants because they are children? So we need to consider subtree. The tree is hierarchical; removing a node removes its entire subtree. So we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one node outside the subtree of the node we are removing that has that symbol, or the node itself is not the only occurrence. Also if the node's symbol is not required, and its children might have required symbols, but those children are in its subtree, so removing the parent would remove those required symbols too. So we must check that after removing the subtree rooted at the node, all required symbols are still present in the remaining tree. That is: for each required symbol, there exists at least one node with that symbol outside the subtree. That includes nodes that are not descendants and also nodes that are ancestors? But ancestors are above, so they are outside. So we need to compute for each node the set of symbols in its subtree? Or simply do a global count of each symbol, and for a node we check if the count of each required symbol minus the number of occurrences of that symbol in the subtree (including the node) is >=1. So we need to precompute subtree symbol counts.\n\nAlso \"Never remove the root\" - root node id is \"root\" from example? Could be any, but root has parent null. So we can identify root. Also \"a required symbol\" means node whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed. So we can filter out nodes that are not removable, or are root, or have symbol in required_symbols.\n\nTiebreak: at equal depth, use node ID as stable tie break. That means sort by depth descending, then by node ID ascending (since node IDs are strings, compare lexicographically). But the description says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So stable tie break means when depths are equal, order by node ID. It doesn't specify ascending or descending, but typically ascending.\n\nReturn sorted retained IDs (presumably sorted ascending) and actual removal order (the order in which nodes were removed). So we need to process nodes in sorted order, and for each, if conditions met, remove it and add its ID to removed list. After processing all, the remaining nodes are retained, sort their IDs.\n\nWe also need to consider that removal order might affect subsequent removals (e.g., if a parent is removed later, its children are already removed? Actually if we process deeper first, children are considered before parents. So if a child is removable and meets conditions, we remove it. Then when we consider the parent, it might have lost that child (already removed), so it might become removable if its remaining children are also removed. So the algorithm is top-down? Actually deeper first means we go from leaves upward. That is typical for Perses reduction. So we need to iterate nodes sorted by depth descending (leaves first). For each node, if it is removable and not root and symbol not required, and if removing its subtree would still leave all required symbols (i.e., each required symbol appears outside that subtree >=1), and if it has no retained children (i.e., all its children have been removed already? But since we process deeper first, children are processed before parent. So we can check if any child node ID is still in retained set? Or we can track a set of removed IDs. At the time we consider a node, its children have already been processed. If a child was not removed (i.e., retained), then the parent cannot be removed because it has retained children. So we need to check if any child is still retained. That's \"never remove a node with retained children\". So we need to know which nodes are retained. We'll maintain a set removed_ids. Initially empty. Then for each node in sorted order, we check conditions: node is removable, not root, node.symbol not in required_symbols, and after removal of its subtree, all required symbols still present, and no child of this node is in the retained set (i.e., child id not in removed_ids). If all true, we add node.id to removed_ids and also add to list removed_order. But careful: the subtree condition includes descendants. If we remove a node, descendants are also removed, but we have already processed them (they might have been removed or retained). If they were already removed by prior decisions, that's fine. If they were retained, then removing their parent would also remove them, which violates the condition \"never remove a node with retained children\". So we must not remove a parent if any of its descendants are still retained? Actually the condition says \"retained children\" – immediate children? Or all descendants? The phrasing: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It says \"retained children\" – immediate children. But if an immediate child is retained, we cannot remove the parent. However, if a grandchild is retained but the child is removed, then the parent can be removed? Possibly yes, because the child is already removed, so the parent does not have retained children. But the grandchild is not a direct child; it's in the subtree but if the child is removed, the grandchild is also gone (since subtree removal is all descendants). So we only need to check immediate children. But we need to be careful: when we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree, so any retained nodes deeper would also be removed. But we already processed deeper nodes; if they were retained, then the parent cannot be removed. So we check if any immediate child's id is not in removed_ids (i.e., still retained). If all children are either removed or don't exist, then the node has no retained children.\n\nNow the required symbol condition: we need to ensure that after removing the subtree rooted at this node, each required symbol appears at least once in the remaining tree. Since we are processing nodes in order and removing subtrees, we can precompute the total count of each required symbol in the entire tree. Then for a node, compute the count of that symbol within its subtree (including itself). If total_count - subtree_count >= 1 for all required symbols, then removal is okay. We can compute subtree symbol counts via DFS from root. Build children adjacency list. Then compute subtree counts recursively. Since we cannot mutate input, we can compute in solve function.\n\nAlso note: the root node id is \"root\" in examples, but we should not assume it's always \"root\". Find node with parent null.\n\nAlgorithm steps:\n\n1. Parse nodes into a dict by id.\n2. Build children list: for each node, if parent not null, add node id to children[parent].\n3. Identify root: node with parent null.\n4. Compute subtree symbol counts: a recursive function that returns a dict of symbol counts for subtree rooted at node_id. Use memoization or simple recursion since tree size moderate.\n5. Global required_symbols list from case.\n6. Compute total_counts: from root subtree count.\n7. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by node id ascending (string compare). Use list of node ids sorted accordingly.\n8. Initialize removed_ids set, removed_order list.\n9. For each node_id in sorted order:\n   - node = nodes[node_id]\n   - Skip if not removable (node[\"removable\"] is False)\n   - Skip if node_id == root_id\n   - Skip if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols\n   - Check subtree counts: for each req_sym in required_symbols:\n       if total_counts[req_sym] - subtree_counts[node_id][req_sym] < 1: skip removal\n   - Check immediate children: for each child_id in children.get(node_id, []):\n       if child_id not in removed_ids: skip removal (has retained child)\n   - If all conditions pass:\n       removed_ids.add(node_id)\n       removed_order.append(node_id)\n10. After loop, retained_ids = sorted([nid for nid in nodes if nid not in removed_ids])\n11. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nBut we need to consider that after removing a node, its children are also removed implicitly. But we don't need to add them to removed_ids explicitly because they are already processed? When we process a node, its children are deeper and already processed. If a child was retained, we would not have removed it. But if we later remove the parent, we should also consider those children as removed? However, the condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" prevents that. So no child is retained if we remove parent. So it's consistent.\n\nBut what about a scenario where a child is removed earlier, then later the parent is removed? That's fine. The child's removal already happened. The parent removal doesn't need to re-add child IDs because they are already in removed_ids.\n\nHowever, we need to be careful: when we remove a node, we effectively remove its entire subtree, but we have already processed descendants. Some descendants might have been removed already (if they satisfied conditions) or retained. If they were retained, we cannot remove the parent. So the check ensures that. So we don't need to recursively add descendants.\n\nPotentially, the algorithm might produce a removal order that is not exactly the order of processing because we only add parent removals after children removals. The removal order is the order in which we removed nodes (i.e., the order we process them and decide to remove). Since we process deeper first, children removals come before parent removals. That matches typical reduction order.\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case) that returns a dict with the two arrays.\n\nWe also need to raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case. What constitutes infeasible? Possibly if required_symbols includes a symbol not present in tree at all? Or if root is removable? We should validate: root must exist, required_symbols must be subset of symbols present? Not necessarily, but if a required symbol is missing, it's impossible to preserve it. So we can check that each required symbol appears at least once in total_counts. Also check that each node has correct depth? The depth is given, we could trust it. But if depth mismatches, we could still proceed. Also nodes must have valid parent references. We can build tree and ensure no cycles? Might be out of scope.\n\nGiven the simplicity, we can do minimal validation: ensure root exists, all nodes have id, parent references exist, etc.\n\nNow we need to output Python source as JSON string under \"implementation\". Must escape properly.\n\nWe'll write code in a block then wrap in JSON.\n\nEdge cases: node IDs are strings, we sort accordingly.\n\nImplementation details: use recursion depth? Tree may be large but within limits. We'll use iterative stack for safety.\n\nAlso note: The payload may have \"_case_nonce\" field, we ignore.\n\nWe'll implement:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in case[\"nodes\"]}\n    required_symbols = case[\"required_symbols\"]\n    # find root\n    roots = [n[\"id\"] for n in nodes.values() if n[\"parent\"] is None]\n    if len(roots) != 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Must have exactly one root\")\n    root_id = roots[0]\n    # build children\n    children = {}\n    for nid, n in nodes.items():\n        p = n[\"parent\"]\n        if p is not None:\n            children.setdefault(p, []).append(nid)\n    # subtree counts via DFS\n    subtree_counts = {}\n    def compute_counts(nid):\n        counts = {}\n        n = nodes[nid]\n        sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n        counts[sym] = counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n        for cid in children.get(nid, []):\n            child_counts = compute_counts(cid)\n            for s, cnt in child_counts.items():\n                counts[s] = counts.get(s, 0) + cnt\n        subtree_counts[nid] = counts\n        return counts\n    total_counts = compute_counts(root_id)\n    # verify required symbols exist\n    for req in required_symbols:\n        if total_counts.get(req, 0) == 0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol '{req}' not present\")\n    # sort nodes\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes.values(), key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    removed_ids = set()\n    removed_order = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # check required symbols after removal\n        sub_counts = subtree_counts[nid]\n        ok = True\n        for req in required_symbols:\n            if total_counts[req] - sub_counts.get(req, 0) < 1:\n                ok = False\n                break\n        if not ok:\n            continue\n        # check no retained immediate children\n        has_retained_child = False\n        for cid in children.get(nid, []):\n            if cid not in removed_ids:\n                has_retained_child = True\n                break\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # remove\n        removed_ids.add(nid)\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    retained_ids = sorted([nid for nid in nodes if nid not in removed_ids])\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids\n    }\n\nCheck with given fixtures: The payloads have same structure. We can test mentally but not needed.\n\nNow produce JSON output with implementation key.\n\nWe must ensure the Python source is a single string properly escaped. We'll write the source without triple quotes, escape newlines as \\n, quotes as \\\", backslashes as \\\\. Use JSON serialization.\n\nWe'll produce in final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":4290,"total_tokens":6459,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3662},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}